ry flag to enable automatic function calling in vLLM is `--enable-auto-tool-choice`."}, {"question_id": "b5191cd4-9b74-4211-9e60-6272498deae3", "question": "How do you apply chat templates to prompts in vLLM?", "retrieved_sources": [{"file_path": "data/raw/vllm-0.10.1/vllm/entrypoints/openai/api_server.py", "first_character_index": 37565, "last_character_index": 38239}], "answer": "To apply chat templates to prompts in vLLM, you can use the `tokenizer.apply_chat_template` method. This method takes a list of prompts and applies the chat template to each one, which is useful for generating responses based on the model's training data."}, {"question_id": "db4f05fe-78bf-402f-b02f-630d0a3959c3", "question": "What is the first step to implement a basic vLLM model?", "retrieved_sources": [{"file_path": "data/raw/vllm-0.10.1/docs/contributing/model/basic.md", "first_character_index": 7619, "last_character_index": 7704}], "answer": "The first step to implement a basic vLLM model is to clone the PyTorch model code from the source repository. For example, vLLM's [OPT model](gh-file:vllm/model_executor/models/opt.py) was adapted from HuggingFace's [modeling_opt.py](https://github.com/huggingface/transformers/blob/main/src/transformers/models/opt/modeling_opt.py) file."}, {"question_id": "4e658668-9e1d-4068-ae99-1f77bab83523", "question": "What flag must be explicitly passed when serving VLM2Vec-Full model in vLLM to run it in embedding mode?", "retrieved_sources": [{"file_path": "data/raw/vllm-0.10.1/docs/usage/security.md", "first_character_index": 3354, "last_character_index": 4017}], "answer": "The flag that must be explicitly passed when serving the VLM2Vec-Full model in vLLM to run it in embedding mode is `--embedding`."}], "k": 100}